## Security & Compliance

- **[CaptiveCrunch: Midnight Blizzard Targets Travelers Worldwide for Malware Delivery and Credential Theft](https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/security/blog/2026/07/31/captivecrunch-midnight-blizzard-targets-travelers-worldwide-for-malware-delivery-and-credential-theft/)** [GA] - Storm-2945, a Midnight Blizzard sub-cluster, has been compromising hotel sign-in portals since May 2026 to push malware and harvest credentials from guests. Review your conditional access policies for sign-ins from hospitality network ranges, ensure MFA is enforced with phishing-resistant methods for traveling users, and push the threat intel to your SOC and travel security teams.

- **[Secure by Default: Trusted Launch as Default is Now Generally Available](https://techcommunity.microsoft.com/t5/microsoft-security-community/secure-by-default-trusted-launch-as-default-is-now-generally/ba-p/4541672)** [GA] - New Azure Gen2 VMs deployed via Portal, PowerShell, and CLI now get Secure Boot and vTPM enabled by default at no extra cost. IaC pipelines using ARM templates, Bicep, Terraform, or SDKs require a one-time registration to activate this default; complete that registration before your next automated deployment cycle.

- **[Why AI Agents May Require a New Security Operations Model](https://techcommunity.microsoft.com/t5/microsoft-security-community/why-ai-agents-may-require-a-new-security-operations-model/ba-p/4542532)** [GA] - A compromised AI agent can do what a compromised endpoint and a compromised identity do simultaneously, while also reasoning, invoking tools, and chaining to other agents at machine speed. This post is essential reading for SOC architects and should inform your detection, response, and agent governance planning.

- **[More Control Over Web Grounding with Domain Exclusion for Microsoft 365 Copilot](https://techcommunity.microsoft.com/t5/microsoft-365-copilot-blog/more-control-over-web-grounding-with-domain-exclusion-for/ba-p/4540151)** [GA] - Admins can now specify domains to exclude from Copilot's web search grounding, keeping responses aligned with organizational policy and trusted source strategies. Configure exclusions in the Microsoft 365 admin center now, especially for regulated industries where web source provenance matters for compliance.

- **[The Microsoft AI and Agent Platform: The Platform Behind Intelligent Agents](https://techcommunity.microsoft.com/t5/microsoft-security-community/the-microsoft-ai-and-agent-platform-the-platform-behind/ba-p/4539060)** [GA] - This post outlines the governance, trust, and enterprise readiness foundations in Microsoft's AI and agent platform. Useful as a vendor alignment reference when evaluating agent deployment architectures and explaining the governance stack to compliance stakeholders.

- **[Better Security Starts with Better Questions](https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/security/blog/2026/07/29/better-security-starts-with-better-questions/)** [GA] - A thought leadership piece on using AI-assisted inquiry and human judgment to build more resilient security programs. Low on operational detail but useful for security leadership positioning and framing AI's role in your security decision-making process.

- **[Assign a CVE to GitHub Copilot, Get a Pull Request](https://www.youtube.com/shorts/Q7BQG2_WDzY)** [GA] - Defender for Cloud can now surface a container CVE recommendation, generate a GitHub issue, assign it to GitHub Copilot coding agent, and receive a draft PR fixing the CVE, with resolution syncing back to Defender. This closes the detection-to-fix loop for container supply chain vulnerabilities; evaluate whether your DevSecOps workflow can adopt this pattern.

- **[This Policy Blocked an Image With 415 CVEs](https://www.youtube.com/shorts/hYI3T0UnXKg)** [GA] - Defender for Cloud security policies can now deny deployment of container images that violate CVE thresholds at the Kubernetes namespace level. If you run AI application workloads in containers, define and scope a zero-tolerance CVE policy to your AI namespace as a supply chain control.

- **[CVE-2026-66803: Azure Cosmos DB Remote Code Execution Vulnerability](https://msrc.microsoft.com/update-guide/vulnerability/CVE-2026-66803)** [GA] - Improper access control in Azure Cosmos DB allows an unauthenticated network attacker to execute code. Review the MSRC advisory, assess tenant exposure, and apply any available mitigations immediately if your applications depend on Cosmos DB.

- **[CVE-2026-54128: Windows DHCP Client Remote Code Execution Vulnerability](https://msrc.microsoft.com/update-guide/vulnerability/CVE-2026-54128)** [GA] - Acknowledgment updated on this Windows DHCP Client RCE. Confirm your Windows endpoints are current on patches that address this vulnerability, particularly for devices on untrusted networks.

- **[CVE-2026-55129: Microsoft Office Remote Code Execution Vulnerability](https://msrc.microsoft.com/update-guide/vulnerability/CVE-2026-55129)** [GA] - Acknowledgment updated on this Office RCE. Verify Office update compliance across your fleet and confirm Defender for Endpoint telemetry shows no exploitation attempts.

- **[CVE-2026-56197: Windows Admin Center (WAC) Remote Code Execution Vulnerability](https://msrc.microsoft.com/update-guide/vulnerability/CVE-2026-56197)** [GA] - Acknowledgment updated on this WAC RCE. Ensure Windows Admin Center deployments are on a patched version and that WAC access is restricted to authorized admin networks.

- **[CVE-2026-50416: Win32k Information Disclosure Vulnerability](https://msrc.microsoft.com/update-guide/vulnerability/CVE-2026-50416)** [GA] - Acknowledgment updated for this Win32k information disclosure. Confirm Windows endpoint patch compliance for the fix covering this CVE.

- **[CVE-2026-50341: Windows NTFS Information Disclosure Vulnerability](https://msrc.microsoft.com/update-guide/vulnerability/CVE-2026-50341)** [GA] - Acknowledgment updated for this NTFS information disclosure. Verify patching for affected Windows versions is current across your managed fleet.

- **[CVE-2026-50493: DirectX Graphics Kernel Elevation of Privilege Vulnerability](https://msrc.microsoft.com/update-guide/vulnerability/CVE-2026-50493)** [GA] - Acknowledgment updated for this DirectX Graphics Kernel EoP. EoP vulnerabilities are frequently chained with initial access exploits; ensure this patch is prioritized in your next update cycle.

- **[CVE-2026-24304: Azure Resource Manager Elevation of Privilege Vulnerability](https://msrc.microsoft.com/update-guide/vulnerability/CVE-2026-24304)** [GA] - Informational update only for this ARM EoP CVE. No action change from prior guidance; confirm your ARM RBAC posture and least-privilege assignments are in good standing.

- **[Chromium CVE-2026-13032: Use After Free in WebGL](https://msrc.microsoft.com/update-guide/vulnerability/CVE-2026-13032)** [GA] - Chrome-assigned CVE addressed in the Microsoft Edge Chromium update. Confirm Edge is current on managed endpoints; Intune or your software update tool should show compliance against the latest Edge build.

- **[Chromium CVE-2026-13030: Uninitialized Use in GPU](https://msrc.microsoft.com/update-guide/vulnerability/CVE-2026-13030)** [GA] - Chrome-assigned CVE addressed in Microsoft Edge Chromium. Same guidance as above; ensure Edge patch compliance across your fleet.

- **[Chromium CVE-2026-13028: Use After Free in WebGL](https://msrc.microsoft.com/update-guide/vulnerability/CVE-2026-13028)** [GA] - Chrome-assigned CVE addressed in Microsoft Edge Chromium. Three WebGL and GPU CVEs this cycle; verify Edge is fully patched on all managed and shared devices.

- **[What Zero Trust Actually Means](https://www.youtube.com/shorts/Lf2KEXY1WEg)** [GA] - A concise Microsoft Mechanics explainer covering the three Zero Trust principles: verify explicitly, enforce least privilege, and assume breach. Useful onboarding material for new team members or stakeholder education sessions.

